## 10. INPUT AND CONTROLS

### 10.1 Principles
1. **Instant response:** act on `pointerdown` and `keydown`, never on `click`. Target under 50 ms from input to the tank reacting.
2. **Learn in 10 seconds:** each style fits on one card with an animated demo.
3. **No misfires:** HUD buttons never fire the cannon; touches never become browser gestures.
4. **Comfortable thumbs:** controls can be moved, resized and mirrored.
5. **Every player chooses:** control style is per player and saved on the device.
6. **One game logic:** all styles produce the same input for the simulation (10.7), so rules and balance stay the same.

### 10.2 Platform input rules
- One InputManager. It turns raw events into logical actions (5.2). Use Pointer Events with pointer capture. Clear all inputs on window blur, tab hidden or orientation change.
- Prevent default for game keys, wheel and context menu **only while a match is running**. Menus keep normal browser behavior (text selection in chat, scrolling lists).
- Use `KeyboardEvent.code` (physical keys) so WASD works on every layout; show key labels with `navigator.keyboard.getLayoutMap()` when available.
- Key remapping in Settings with conflict detection; reserved: `Esc` and `F11`. A **Key test** screen shows which keys register (helps with keyboard ghosting).
- CSS on the game container: `touch-action: none; user-select: none; -webkit-touch-callout: none; overscroll-behavior: none;`.

### 10.3 The three control styles
| Style | Idea | Best for |
|---|---|---|
| **ARCADE** (original feel) | The tank drives forward while you hold the drive input. Firing makes the tank recoil and turn | Local Party (one button), beginners, fans of the reference game |
| **STANDARD** (tank controls) | Forward, reverse, rotate left and right, fire | PC players who want control; **recommended on PC** |
| **STEER** (point and go) | You point where the tank should face; it turns toward that and drives | Phones (Twin-Thumb), gamepad sticks, mouse players |

### 10.4 PC: keyboard and mouse
**A. Arcade (Space + Click)** (pre-selected in the first-run picker)
| Input | Action |
|---|---|
| **Hold Space** | Tank drives forward at once (no delay) |
| **Left mouse click** (anywhere on the stage) | Fire one shell + recoil turn (shot 1 right, shot 2 left, shot 3 right...) |
| **A / D** or **Left / Right arrows** | Turn assist (hull turns 160 deg/s). Setting "Turn assist": ON on PC, OFF in Local Party |
| Right click, wheel | Ignored in a match |
**B. Standard (WASD)** (badge: "Best control")
| Input | Action |
|---|---|
| **W** or **Up** | Drive forward (210 px/s) |
| **S** or **Down** | Reverse (120 px/s) |
| **A / D** or **Left / Right** | Rotate the hull (220 deg/s) |
| **Space** or **Left click** | Fire (hold = keeps firing at the cooldown while ammo lasts; setting "Hold to rapid fire") |
| Recoil turn | OFF in this style |
**C. Mouse Steer**
| Input | Action |
|---|---|
| **Move the mouse** | The tank turns toward the cursor (220 deg/s) |
| **Hold Right click** or **W** | Drive forward; **S** reverses |
| **Left click** or **Space** | Fire |
| Cursor | A small ring at the cursor shows the target heading; a thin line shows the barrel direction |
**D. Local Party on one PC (one key per player, Arcade only):** P1 `Q`, P2 `P`, P3 `Z`, P4 `M`. **Hold = drive, quick tap = fire + recoil turn** (tap time 0.2 s, hold delay 0.1 s). Remappable.

**PC shortcuts**
| Key | Action |
|---|---|
| `Esc` | Pause menu |
| `Tab` (hold) | Scoreboard overlay |
| `F` or `F11` | Fullscreen (`F` is off in Local Party because of key use) |
| `Ctrl + M` | Mute |
| `?` (Shift + /) | Controls overlay (the player's current style) |
| `Enter` | Open chat (lobby only) |
| `Ctrl/Cmd + K` | Command palette (menus only) |
**Mouse rules**
- The cursor stays visible and hides after 2 s without movement during a match; it returns on movement.
- A click fires only when the click target is the game canvas. Clicks on HUD buttons, the pause menu or chat never fire.
- Right-click menu and text selection are blocked **only during a match**.
- When the mouse leaves the window or the window loses focus: release all inputs, the tank stops; the aim ring stays at the last point.
- Before a match starts, blur the focused element so Space can never press a focused button.

### 10.5 Phone and tablet: touch
All touch layouts use **landscape**. Controls stay at least 24 px from the screen edges (avoids system swipe gestures) and respect safe-area insets.
**A. Twin-Thumb** (recommended on phones, STEER style)
```
+-------------------------------------------------------------+
| [Red 0]        1:42        [Blue 1]              [II] [mute] |
| ammo o o o o o                                               |
|    (  floating  )                                [  FIRE  ]  |
|    (   stick    )                                           |
+-------------------------------------------------------------+
```
| Part | Rule |
|---|---|
| Stick zone | The left 45% of the screen. The stick appears **where the first touch lands** (floating, default) or sits at a fixed spot (setting) |
| Stick size | base 140 px diameter (setting 100 to 180), knob 64 px, max drag 60 px, dead zone 12% |
| Behavior | The tank turns toward the stick direction (220 deg/s) and drives at speed = stick strength. When the stick angle is more than 60 degrees from the hull, speed is 60% so the tank pivots first. Release = stops |
| FIRE button | Bottom-right, 110 px diameter (setting 80 to 150), hit area 16 px bigger than drawn. **Tap** = one shell. **Hold** = keeps firing at the cooldown while ammo lasts (setting) |
| Opacity | 45% idle, 85% pressed (setting 20% to 100%) |
| Haptics | 10 ms vibration per shot, 25 ms when hit (setting, where supported) |
| Left-handed | Swap sides (mirror layout) |
| Aim assist | Light (default on touch): when an enemy is within 10 degrees of the barrel and 500 px away, the hull nudges up to 4 degrees toward it per shot. Never fires by itself. Setting Off / Light |
| Auto-fire (accessibility) | Optional: fires when an enemy is within 5 degrees in front. Default OFF |
**B. Tank Pad** (STANDARD style with buttons): left thumb has **left** and **right** rotate buttons; right thumb has **drive** (hold) and **FIRE**. Button size 100 px. Reverse is not on this pad.
**C. One-Thumb Arcade** (ARCADE style): touch and **hold anywhere** to drive (a faint ring shows at the touch point); a **quick tap** (under 0.2 s) fires and recoil-turns. Good for one-handed play.
**D. Local Party on one screen:** each player touches their **corner button** (120 px or larger on screen). Hold = drive, quick tap = fire + recoil turn. Up to 4 fingers; each finger is captured by its button.
**Touch behaviors**
- Pointer Events with pointer capture; each pointer is independent. Online and Solo use 2 pointers (stick + fire); extra pointers are ignored. Local Party uses up to 4.
- A finger that starts on the stick stays the stick even if it slides over FIRE (and the other way round).
- Touches on the pause or mute buttons never reach the game.
- One-time tip: "Swiping from the screen edge may leave the game."

### 10.6 Gamepad
| Input | Action |
|---|---|
| Left stick | STEER (same as Twin-Thumb; dead zone 0.2; calibration slider in Settings) |
| `A` or `RT` | Fire (hold = rapid fire) |
| D-pad | Standard-style drive and rotate (optional) |
| `Start` | Pause |
| Local Party | one pad per player; any face button = Arcade hold/tap |
Hot-plug works (toast "Gamepad 2 connected").

### 10.7 Canonical input for the simulation (all styles produce this)
```ts
type TankInput = {
  seq: number;
  throttle: number;        // -1..1  (ARCADE: 0 or 1; STANDARD: -1..1; STEER: 0..1)
  turn: number;            // -1..1  (ARCADE turn assist and STANDARD; 0 for STEER)
  heading: number | null;  // degrees 0..360 for STEER; null otherwise
  fire: number;            // counter, +1 per shot request
};
```
The client sends this at 30 Hz (or on change; compact array form `[seq,throttle,turn,heading,fire]`). The control **style** is sent once with the player profile (`setProfile`) and again in `matchStart`; the server validates it.

### 10.8 How each style moves the tank (shared sim, `config.ts`)
| Rule | ARCADE | STANDARD | STEER |
|---|---|---|---|
| Forward speed | 210 px/s | 210 px/s | 210 px/s x strength |
| Reverse | none | 120 px/s | none (it turns instead) |
| Turn | recoil 30 degrees per shot + optional assist 160 deg/s | 220 deg/s | 220 deg/s toward `heading` |
| Acceleration | 0.1 s to full speed, 0.1 s to stop | same | same |
| Fire | tap (one-button) or separate fire input | fire input | fire input |
| Recoil turn on fire | ON (host option) | OFF | OFF |
The server caps speed and turn rate per style so a modified client cannot move faster, and ignores `heading` for styles that do not use it.

### 10.9 Defaults, first-run picker and fairness
- **Defaults:** PC = **ARCADE (Space + Click)**, Touch = **Twin-Thumb**, Gamepad = STEER, Local Party = Arcade one-button (fixed). In `config.ts`: `DEFAULT_STYLE = { pc: 'ARCADE', touch: 'STEER_TWIN_THUMB', pad: 'STEER' }` (change `pc` to `'STANDARD'` to make Standard the default).
- On first launch (and any time from Settings) the **Control Setup** screen (8.13) shows 3 cards for the detected device. "Try it" opens a 20 s practice range. The choice is saved in `oa.settings.controls.<device>`.
- Host options: **Control styles allowed** (All or Standard only) and **Aim assist allowed** (On or Off). Each lobby player row shows a style icon. A player can change style in the pause menu; online it applies from the next respawn.

### 10.10 Why these controls (recommendation)
1. Two thumbs (stick + FIRE) give phone players a precise tank; steering by recoil alone costs ammo and feels random.
2. WASD + Space/Click is what PC players already know, so a colleague plays with no teaching.
3. Mouse Steer makes aiming very fast for mouse users.
4. The Arcade style keeps the original one-button feel and is the only style that works for 4 people on one screen.
5. One shared input format keeps rules and anti-cheat simple, and new games reuse the same InputManager.

