## 11. NETWORKING AND ROOMS

**Server:** one Node.js process serves the built client AND the WebSocket on the same port (`process.env.PORT`, path `/ws`, health `/healthz`, listen on `0.0.0.0`). Rooms live in memory. Each room runs inside try/catch so one crash never stops other rooms.

### 11.1 Rooms
- 4-letter code from the allowed letters (no I, O, L), generated until unique and not on a small blocklist of unwanted words. Stores `gameId`, room name, visibility, password hash, max players, options, host.
- States `LOBBY -> LOADING -> COUNTDOWN -> PLAYING -> RESULTS -> LOBBY`.
- Deleted 60 s after the last human leaves, or at once when the host closes it.
- Host migration to the earliest-joined human; **transfer host** on request; kick blocks rejoin for 60 s; **ban** blocks for the life of the room.
- Reconnect within 60 s with a `playerToken` in sessionStorage; ping every 2 s; silent connections dropped after 10 s.
- **Open rooms list:** the server keeps a list of open rooms `{code, name, gameId, hostName, players, max, status, hasPassword}`. It is sent on request and pushed every 5 s to clients on the Join screen. Private rooms are never included.
- **Quick Join:** the server picks an open room of the game with a free seat, status LOBBY, no password, the most players first.

### 11.2 Messages (generic, `/platform/shared/protocol.ts`)
- Client to server: `listGames, listRooms, quickJoin{gameId}, createRoom{gameId,options}, joinRoom{code,password,asSpectator}, leaveRoom, closeRoom, setRoomOptions, setProfile{nickname,color,icon,controlStyle}, setTeam, shuffleTeams, setReady, hostSettings, startMatch{force}, takeSeat, joinMatch, kick{id}, ban{id}, transferHost{id}, input{t,payload}, chat, rematch, vote, ping`.
- Server to client: `games, rooms, roomJoined, roomUpdate, loading, countdown, matchStart{mapId,seed,settings,styles}, snapshot{t,payload}, event{t,payload}, matchEnd, error, pong`.
The platform never reads `payload`; the game defines it. For Tank Arena the input payload is `TankInput` (10.7).

### 11.3 Real-time loop (games with `tickRate` > 0)
- Server simulation at the game's tick (Tank Arena 30 Hz), snapshots at 20 Hz, reliable events at once.
- Client-side prediction for the player's own object, 100 ms interpolation for the rest (extrapolate at most 200 ms). Hits, damage and scores come only from the server. Optional lag compensation up to 150 ms.
- **Compact snapshots for 12 players:** a snapshot holds only tanks as arrays of rounded integers `[id, x, y, angle, flags, ammo, power]`, plus flags, power boxes, scores and the timer. **Shells are not in snapshots**: clients simulate them from `shot` events (spawn position, angle, tick). Brick damage, destruction, pickups and respawns are events. Target: under 15 KB/s per client at 12 players, hard warning at 40 KB/s. Optional binary encoding later.

### 11.4 Capacity and limits (`/platform/shared/config.ts`)
| Limit | Value |
|---|---|
| Players per room | the game's max (Tank Arena 12) |
| Spectators per room | 20 |
| Rooms per server | 50 |
| Connections per server | 600 |
| Connections per IP | 40 (offices share one IP) |
| Create room | 5 per minute per socket |
| Join attempts | 10 per minute per socket (wrong password counts) |
| Messages | 120 per second per socket (close at 200); one message at most 4 KB |
Target on one 1 vCPU instance: 10 rooms with 12 players each, server tick time under 10 ms, CPU under 70% (measured by the load test).

### 11.5 Validation and safety
Clamp every value to its range; nickname 1 to 14 characters without `<` `>`; room name 1 to 24 characters, same filtering; chat 80 characters and 1 per second; unknown message types ignored; no personal data stored; passwords hashed and compared on the server only; control style must be one the game declares and the host allows; host-only messages (`hostSettings, startMatch, kick, ban, transferHost, closeRoom, setRoomOptions`) are rejected from others.

### 11.6 Debug and tools
`?lag=150&loss=3` (simulate a bad network), `?debug=1` (overlay with ping, tick, snapshot rate and size, prediction error, input latency, entity count, FPS, collision shapes). `npm run loadtest -- --rooms 5 --players 12` runs `/tools/loadtest.ts` (headless bot clients that join, ready up, play and leave; prints tick time, CPU, bandwidth per client and any errors).

### 11.7 Plan B
Only if a WebSocket server cannot run: host-authoritative peer-to-peer (PeerJS/WebRTC) with the host browser running the shared simulation. Tell the user it is less reliable behind strict office firewalls and is limited to about 6 players.

